# Derived case artifacts — regenerated, never hand-edited.
#
# `RULE_CASES.json` alone was 135,000 lines, and every PR that touched a rule
# carried it. Nobody can find a nine-line behaviour change in that, so the
# review it was supposed to enable never happened.
#
# What stays in git is what cannot be derived:
#   benchmarks/cases/registry.json    hand-authored — the accountability register
#   benchmarks/budgets/*.json         ratchet baselines — the shrink-only contract
#   benchmarks/*_PHILOSOPHY.md, VERDICT_LEDGER.md, CASE_PHILOSOPHY.md   written
#
# Regenerating is also what makes the locks honest. `sealed-vs-open-lock` read
# the COMMITTED ledger, so it verified whatever was last generated rather than
# what the tests currently say; it now regenerates first, and a poisoned
# artifact no longer changes its verdict.
#
#   npm run rule-cases          # RULE_CASES.{json,md}
#   npm run case-registry       # CASE_REGISTRY.md
#   npm run audit:spellings     # SPELLING_MISSES.{json,md}
#   npm run probe:name-dependence
benchmarks/RULE_CASES.json
benchmarks/RULE_CASES.md
benchmarks/SPELLING_MISSES.json
benchmarks/SPELLING_MISSES.md
benchmarks/NAME_DEPENDENCE.json
benchmarks/CASE_REGISTRY.md
benchmarks/CANDIDATES.md

# The sabotage fixture written by benchmarks/__tests__/sealed-vs-open-lock.test.ts.
# It is deleted in a `finally`, but a killed process leaves it behind — and it
# deliberately contains a contradictory FN marker, so committing it would wedge
# the ledger for everyone. Ignored so `git add -A` cannot pick it up.
packages/*/src/tests/__coherence-probe.test.ts
